{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794595762,"nonce":"yfjeiFs4PkqbQ2I4UHNN7X0jR+A=","message":"Diagnosed with temporary timestamps along the wake path (removed): the timer thread, winit's user_event, the redraw, about_to_wait, and the window events. flip_window_check on macOS, 22 runs.\n\nTimer thread: on time. The send is 0 to 4 ms past its deadline, 20 ms at worst. wake_at and its condvar thread are not the late part.\n\nStartup: every run has one gap of 0.2 to 0.4 s after the first frame. The first frame's handler finishes in 11 to 20 ms, the loop reaches about_to_wait and sleeps, and the timer's wakes sit undelivered. They reach user_event 40 to 100 ms after the window's Focused(true) event, which is the first thing the loop receives. So the loop is waiting for macOS to make the app active; nothing is delivered before that. Turning window animations off (-NSAutomaticWindowAnimationsEnabled NO) changed nothing. This accounts for the 657, 604, 208 and 177 ms gaps in the runs that opened this issue: they came right after the second frame.\n\nMid-run: one gap in about 3 minutes of running, 296 ms at t=10.4 s in one of eight 15 s runs. Not tied to a reorder, and the timer was not the cause in the startup cases; not measured for this one (no wake_lag stamps were running). Everything else was under 80 ms. Not reproduced.\n\nNoted in the book (performance.md, tracing section) so the startup gap is not read as a slow frame. Leaving this open for the mid-run case; nothing else is actionable, since the startup wait is the OS's.","files":null}]}